/*
 * A15 host devices: the vibrator HAL the Redroid 15 image expects but does not ship.
 *
 * Why this file exists
 * --------------------
 * frameworks/base/libs/vibratorservice (linked into libandroid_servers.so as
 * "android::vibrator::HalController") connects in this order (disassembly of
 * connectHal, libvibratorservice.so 0x11680):
 *
 *   1. AServiceManager_isDeclared("<IVibrator descriptor>/default")
 *        true  -> AServiceManager_waitForService(...) + IVibrator::fromBinder
 *                 => AidlHalWrapper (the AIDL HAL is used, nothing blocks)
 *   2. otherwise -> V1_0::IVibrator::getService("default") which loops forever
 *                 printing "Waited one second for android.hardware.vibrator@1.0
 *                 ::IVibrator/default" while no HIDL service is registered.
 *
 * The image ships the AIDL *client* stack (system/lib64/android.hardware.vibrator
 * -V3-ndk.so, which also exports BnVibrator/BnVibratorManager) but no vibrator
 * service binary: vendor/bin/hw has ten services and none of them is a vibrator.
 * So the project supplies the missing HAL, exactly like it supplies the gralloc
 * / hwcomposer modules behind the ROM's composer binary.
 *
 * A device without a vibrator is a legal Android configuration: VibratorManager
 * Service accepts an empty id list and registers zero vibrators. This HAL
 * therefore reports no vibrator ids and no capabilities. Wiring the ids to the
 * host vibrator (A9's HostVibratorHal socket protocol) is a separate change.
 *
 * Not HIDL: a HIDL server needs headers generated by hidl-gen from
 * hardware/interfaces, and that toolchain does not exist in this workspace
 * (coding-log 2026-10-29, round 69 section 2). The AIDL interfaces are meant to
 * be consumed from the NDK, and the image exports the service-side classes.
 */
